1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,692
Daily portfolio returns, credit scores, measurement errors, heights. Plot them

2
00:00:04,792 --> 00:00:08,011
and the same bell shape keeps appearing. It appears so

3
00:00:08,111 --> 00:00:11,882
often because adding up many small independent effects tends to

4
00:00:11,982 --> 00:00:15,016
produce it, a story we'll make precise later in the

5
00:00:15,116 --> 00:00:17,166
course. Today: how to work with it.

6
00:00:19,266 --> 00:00:21,995
The key fact about the normal family is that

7
00:00:22,095 --> 00:00:25,854
there's really only one normal curve, shifted and stretched.

8
00:00:26,950 --> 00:00:30,880
The normal density has two parameters. Mu locates it: change mu

9
00:00:30,980 --> 00:00:34,974
and the whole curve slides left or right without changing shape.

10
00:00:36,066 --> 00:00:39,035
Sigma spreads it: make sigma bigger and the curve

11
00:00:39,135 --> 00:00:42,166
gets wider and flatter, smaller and it gets narrow

12
00:00:42,266 --> 00:00:45,109
and tall. The area underneath always stays one.

13
00:00:46,200 --> 00:00:49,280
Credit scores at a bank are roughly normal, with mean

14
00:00:49,380 --> 00:00:53,180
seven hundred and standard deviation fifty. Subtract the mean and

15
00:00:53,280 --> 00:00:56,660
divide by the standard deviation, and you get the standard

16
00:00:56,760 --> 00:00:59,840
normal, Z, with mean zero and standard deviation one.

17
00:00:59,933 --> 00:01:03,633
And the areas match. The chance of a score above seven

18
00:01:03,733 --> 00:01:07,856
seventy-five is exactly the chance that Z is above one point

19
00:01:07,956 --> 00:01:11,868
five: about six point seven percent. So one table, or one

20
00:01:11,968 --> 00:01:16,865
R function, answers questions about every normal distribution there is.

21
00:01:18,950 --> 00:01:21,104
Because every normal is the same curve,

22
00:01:21,204 --> 00:01:23,473
three numbers are worth knowing by heart.

23
00:01:23,566 --> 00:01:26,614
Within one standard deviation of the mean: about

24
00:01:26,714 --> 00:01:30,943
sixty-eight percent. Within two: about ninety-five percent. Within

25
00:01:31,043 --> 00:01:35,009
three: ninety-nine point seven. Compare that with Tchebysheff,

26
00:01:35,109 --> 00:01:38,944
who could only promise seventy-five and eighty-nine percent:

27
00:01:39,044 --> 00:01:42,091
knowing the shape buys you much sharper answers.

28
00:01:43,183 --> 00:01:48,061
One last use, straight from the risk desk. A portfolio's daily return is

29
00:01:48,161 --> 00:01:52,969
roughly normal. What loss is exceeded only on the worst five percent of

30
00:01:53,069 --> 00:01:57,256
days? Find the point that cuts off five percent of area in the

31
00:01:57,356 --> 00:02:01,473
left tail: minus one point six four five standard deviations.

32
00:02:01,566 --> 00:02:05,801
Translate back to returns: mean plus minus one point six four

33
00:02:05,901 --> 00:02:10,348
five times sigma, about minus one point nine percent. That's the

34
00:02:10,448 --> 00:02:15,038
ninety-five percent value at risk. It rests entirely on the normal

35
00:02:15,138 --> 00:02:20,509
assumption, which real returns, with their fatter tails, don't always honour.

36
00:02:21,600 --> 00:02:25,695
So: there is really one normal curve. Mu shifts it and

37
00:02:25,795 --> 00:02:31,366
sigma stretches it. Standardising turns any normal into the standard one,

38
00:02:31,466 --> 00:02:37,892
so one table answers every question. And sixty-eight, ninety-five, ninety-nine point

39
00:02:37,992 --> 00:02:42,476
seven tell you, at a glance, how the probability is spread.

